            <div xml:id="d532">

                <pb facs="0638" n="566" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg566"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 1144/143.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Leishman to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Embassy</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Pera</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1909-04-17"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">April 17, 1909</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Leishman says population remains terror stricken, although the excesses of
                    the troops have greatly diminished. He states that it is quite possible that the
                    constitutional party may endeavor to regain the control which has been wrested
                    from them, in which event very serious trouble may be expected, although much
                    depends upon the action of the Third Army Corps, which was the main instrument
                    last summer in bringing about the reestablishment of constitutional government.
                    Mr. Leishman adds that while reports are conflicting, belief that the army corps
                    remains faithful to the constitutional party and is preparing to march on
                    Constantinople is pretty general, and the developments of the next few days will
                    no doubt clear the situation very much.</p>
            </div>
